\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
%
% TRANSFORMATION TObjCaseAssign
< m1:CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ mu1,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                    [ mu2,
                      head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu3],
                      mu4,
                      I1:rel1/T1,
                      mu5 
                    ],
             mu6 
           ]
>

< m :CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ mu1,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                    [ mu2,
                      head/VERB{VERBrec1}[mu3],
                      mu4,
                      I2:rel1/T2,
                      mu5 
                    ],
             mu6 
           ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: rel1 IN [predrel, objrel, indobjrel] AND
           (T1.CAT IN [NPVAR, CNVAR, NP] WITH T1.REC.cases = [] )
       I2: rel1 IN [predrel, objrel, indobjrel] AND
           (T2.CAT IN [NPVAR, CNVAR, NP] WITH accusative IN T2.REC.cases)
      >
<
   SUBRULE (* 1. NPVAR *)
      < T1:NPVAR{NPVARrec1} >
      < T2:NPVAR{NPVARrec2} >

      COMP
         <
          C1: VERBrec1.caseassigner = true AND
              VERBrec1.subc <> beVerb
          A1: NPVARrec2 := NPVARrec1;
              NPVARrec2.cases := [accusative]
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: VERBrec1.caseassigner = true AND
              VERBrec1.subc <> beVerb
          A1: NPVARrec1 := NPVARrec2;
              NPVARrec1.cases := [] 
         >

   SUBRULE (* 2. CNVAR *)
      < T1:CNVAR{CNVARrec1} >
      < T2:CNVAR{CNVARrec2} >

      COMP
         <
          C1: VERBrec1.caseassigner = true AND
              VERBrec1.subc <> beVerb
          A1: CNVARrec2 := CNVARrec1;
              CNVARrec2.cases := [accusative]
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: VERBrec1.caseassigner = true AND
              VERBrec1.subc <> beVerb
          A1: CNVARrec1 := CNVARrec2;
              CNVARrec1.cases := [] 
         >

   SUBRULE (* 3. NP *)
      < T1:NP{NPrec1}[mu7] >
      < T2:NP{NPrec2}[mu8] >

      COMP
         <
          C1: VERBrec1.caseassigner = true AND
              VERBrec1.subc <> beVerb
          A1: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec1, mu7, NPrec2, mu8, [accusative])
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: VERBrec1.caseassigner = true AND
              VERBrec1.subc <> beVerb
          A1: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec2, mu8, NPrec1, mu7, [] )
         >
>

&
\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TObjCaseAssign
\item[* relevant examples:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item predrel: (for NP as a complement coming from NPPROPgrammar; note that
CN/NPVAR identificationals and `be' + NP go through the TBeCaseAssign Rule)\\
x1 elect x2 President (with cases of `President' = [accusative])\\
?x1 act the experienced man (Subject Control)\\
x1 become President (NoControl)
\item objrel: (for NPVAR and CNVAR; for idiom and reflexive NPs; note that 
existentials go through the TBeCaseAssign rule)\\
x1 see x2 ( I saw he $\rightarrow$ I saw him)
\item indobjrel: (for NPVAR and CNVAR; for idiom and reflexive NPs)\\
x1 gave x3 x2 (I gave he a book $\rightarrow$ I gave him a book)
\end{enumerate}
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_
CaseAssignment
\item[* date of creation:] 29-MAR-1988
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch TCaseAssignment1
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To assign accusative case to NPVARs, CNVARs and NPs that 
occur in a predrel, an objrel or an indobjrel, given that the verb is a case 
assigner.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
There is a subrule for NPVAR, one for CNVAR and one for NP.
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
It is assumed that in all passive sentences, the object has become subject or 
is a sentence. Another assumption is that in English, verbs that do not assign 
case
(ergatives) have all moved their objects into subject position too, or have 
received a dummy subject `There'; for the latter case (in which the `object' 
should receive nominative case), a special rule has been written, 
TErgCaseAssign. \\
This also holds for identificational 
constructions: There are some boys on the roof.\\
A final assumption concerns the existence of a robustness rule to take care of 
NPs that are still undefined as to case. They probably should be interpreted as 
[nominative].
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\

\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% TRANSFORMATION TErgBeCaseAssign
< m1:CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ mu1,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                    [ mu2,
                      head/I3::VERB{VERBrec1}[mu3],
                      mu4,
                      I1:rel1/T1,
                      mu5 
                    ],
             mu6 
           ]
>

< m :CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
           [ mu1,
             predrel/VERBP{VERBPrec1}
                    [ mu2,
                      head/I3::VERB{VERBrec1}[mu3],
                      mu4,
                      I2:rel1/T2,
                      mu5 
                    ],
             mu6 
           ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: rel1 IN [predrel, objrel, indobjrel] AND
           (T1.CAT IN [NPVAR, CNVAR, NP] WITH T1.REC.cases = [] )
       I2: rel1 IN [predrel, objrel, indobjrel] AND
           T2.CAT IN [NPVAR, CNVAR, NP] 
       I3: VERBrec1.subc = beVerb OR VERBrec1.caseassigner = false
      >
<
   SUBRULE (* 1. NPVAR *)
      < T1:NPVAR{NPVARrec1} >
      < T2:NPVAR{NPVARrec2} >

      COMP
         <
          C1: NPVARrec1.mood = nowh
          A1: NPVARrec2 := NPVARrec1;
              NPVARrec2.cases := [accusative]
          C2: NPVARrec1.mood = wh
          A2: NPVARrec2 := NPVARrec1;
              NPVARrec2.cases := [nominative]
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: accusative IN NPVARrec2.cases AND
              NPVARrec2.mood = nowh
          A1: NPVARrec1 := NPVARrec2;
              NPVARrec1.cases := [] 
          C2: nominative IN NPVARrec2.cases AND
              NPVARrec2.mood = wh
          A2: NPVARrec1 := NPVARrec2;
              NPVARrec1.cases := [] 
         >

   SUBRULE (* 2. CNVAR *)
      < T1:CNVAR{CNVARrec1} >
      < T2:CNVAR{CNVARrec2} >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true (* CNVAR always wh *)
          A1: CNVARrec2 := CNVARrec1;
              CNVARrec2.cases := [nominative]
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: nominative IN CNVARrec2.cases
          A1: CNVARrec1 := CNVARrec2;
              CNVARrec1.cases := [] 
         >

   SUBRULE (* 3. NP *)
      < T1:NP{NPrec1}[mu7] >
      < T2:NP{NPrec2}[mu8] >

      COMP
         <
          C1: NPrec1.mood = nowh
          A1: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec1, mu7, NPrec2, mu8, [accusative])
          C1: NPrec1.mood = wh
          A1: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec1, mu7, NPrec2, mu8, [nominative])
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: accusative IN NPrec2.cases AND
              NPrec2.mood = nowh
          A1: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec2, mu8, NPrec1, mu7, [] )
          C2: nominative IN NPrec2.cases AND
              NPrec2.mood = wh
          A2: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec2, mu8, NPrec1, mu7, [] )
         >
>

&
\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TErgBeCaseAssign
\item[* relevant examples:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item predrel: (for NP from NPPROPgrammar, but also for CN/NPVAR in case of 
identificationals)\\
x1 be President (with cases of `President' = [accusative])\\
x1 be me (You are me)\\
x1 be who (Who am I)\\
x1 be x2 (he being the man who he is)
x1 become President\\
x1 seem me (?You seem me)\\
x1 seem who (?Who did she seem?)//
\item objrel: (for NPVAR and CNVAR: existentials, and ergatives that have not 
shifted to subject position, introducing the dummy `There')\\
x1 be x2 (who is there on the phone)\\
There appeared x1 in the garden (x1 = some men / who)
\item indobjrel: (for NPVAR and CNVAR: ditransitive ergatives with synIONP\_DONP
)\\
no examples found yet
\end{enumerate}
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_
CaseAssignment
\item[* date of creation:] 2-FEB-1989
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] TObjCaseAssign
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 6/3/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Collapsed the rules for BeVerb and Ergative Case Assignment, since it seems as 
if for ergatives the same restrictions with respect to wh-NPs exist (always 
nominative).
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To assign accusative case to nowh NPVARs and NPs that 
occur in a predrel or an (ind)objrel, given that the verb is `be' or an 
ergative, and nominative 
case for wh NP(VAR)s and CNVAR.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
There is a subrule for NPVAR, one for CNVAR and one for NP.
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:]
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\
It is assumed that non-wh (ind)objs of ergatives are indeed accusative. This 
cannot be checked, since an object that has not shifted to subject position 
must always be indefinite and hence does not show its case (There came three 
men into the room; only allowed in analysis - in generation shift to subject 
position is obligatory: Three men came into the room).
\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
No rule has been written yet to cover structures like {\em That is I, It was he
\/}, so with a nominative non-wh object. Probably, such a rule must be written 
for analysis; it seems as if a condition on predrel VARs suffices to catch all 
possible cases (identificationals).
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

\newpage
\begin{verbatim}
% TRANSFORMATION TSubjCaseAssign
< m1: I3::CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
                [ mu1,
                  I1:subjrel/T1,
                  mu2 
                ]
>

< m : I3::CLAUSE{CLAUSErec1}
                [ mu1,
                  I2:subjrel/T2,
                  mu2 
                ]
>

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: T1.CAT IN [NPVAR, CNVAR, NP] WITH T1.REC.cases = [] 
       I2: T2.CAT IN [NPVAR, CNVAR, NP] WITH nominative IN T2.REC.cases 
       I3: CLAUSErec1.finiteness = finite
      >

<
   SUBRULE
      < T1:NPVAR{NPVARrec1} >
      < T2:NPVAR{NPVARrec2} >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: NPVARrec2 := NPVARrec1;
              NPVARrec2.cases := [nominative]
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: NPVARrec1 := NPVARrec2;
              NPVARrec1.cases := []  
         >

   SUBRULE
      < T1:CNVAR{CNVARrec1} >
      < T2:CNVAR{CNVARrec2} >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: CNVARrec2 := CNVARrec1;
              CNVARrec2.cases := [nominative]
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: CNVARrec1 := CNVARrec2;
              CNVARrec1.cases := []  
         >

   SUBRULE
      < T1:NP{NPrec1}[mu3] >
      < T2:NP{NPrec2}[mu4] >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec1, mu3, NPrec2, mu4, [nominative])
         >

      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: QUOTE_assigncase(NPrec2, mu4, NPrec1, mu3, [] )
         >

>
&
\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] TSubjCaseAssign
\item[* relevant examples:] I gave him a book, She watches everyone, It was him
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] XPPROPtoCLAUSE
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) TC\_
CaseAssignment
\item[* date of creation:] 6-APR-1988
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] Dutch TCaseAssignment2
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:] To assign nominative case to subjects of finite sentences. 
Infinite sentences will get a subject with accusative case, either because of 
the ExceptCaseAssign-rule, or because of the preposition `for' which will be 
entered for forto-sentences in the mooddetermination rules.
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals)
\item[\ \ rejected alternatives:]
\item[\ \ clarification of variables:]
\item[\ \ clarification of the splitting up into subrules:]
There is a subrule for NPVAR, one for CNVAR, and one for NP.
\item[\ \ clarification of the collapsing of rules:] (e.g. clarification of parameters)
\item[\ \ differences analytical/generative version:]
\item[\  \ suggested semantics:] dna
\item[\ \ presuppositions:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item dictionary:\\

\item rules:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[\ \ consequences in the grammar:]
\item[\ \ translation aspects:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate} 
\item within this language:\\

\item in other languages:\\

\end{enumerate}
\item[* relevant literature/documents:]
\item[\ \ remarks:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item problems:\\
English also allows subject complements with a nominative: It was he. 
Nominative case is even obligatory when the complement is modified: It was he 
who came. Following the words {\em but, except, than\/} and {\em as\/} both 
nominative and accusative are allowed when the constituent is in subject 
position or a subject complement: He is more intelligent than she/her; 
Nobody except him/(he) can solve our problems. (Quirk, p.210)\\
It is unclear how all these options should be dealt with. Probably there should 
be a specific action in the PREPPformation subgrammar to postpone 
caseassignment for these preps, and an adaption of the 
assigncase function, so that it checks whether the NP(VAR) in which the PREPP 
occurs is nominative or accusative, and then assign the correct case.
\item modifications:\\

\end{enumerate}
\end{description}

